{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789769832,"nonce":"ziJG46l3hXE6g0VdsXCHbS86YU4=","title":"Two tiers compiled one function's baseline and Cranelift refused the second definition","message":"With the interpreter as the ground tier, a function left for its first call had two ways to its baseline: the stub in its call cell (the first-call compiler, guarded by a per-bead table) and beadie's tier-0 policy fired from the interpreter's tick callback after baseline_threshold calls, which compiled the body through compile_at_tier. Both declared the function under its generation-0 name and the second define_function failed with 'Duplicate definition of identifier: zb_any_hash__HirId(115)', reported as 'a function compiled on first call could not be compiled'. Seen once on nqueens (bead 215) and deterministically on set/dynamic_order.py once zb_any_hash became recursive through set hashing. Fixed by giving the tick callback the same first-call compiler for lazy functions (counting calls itself until the bead has code) and the load-time code for the others, and by ensure_baseline doing the same; the tier-0 policy no longer compiles anything of its own.","files":null}]}